#!/usr/bin/env python3
# gen-libm — regenerate include/kama_libm.h, the musl math a `reproducible-float` program compiles in, from ONE pinned
# musl release.
#
#   tools/gen-libm <a musl source tree>      # emsdk ships one: upstream/emscripten/system/lib/libc/musl
#
# Why it exists. The key promises the same bits on every target, and `std::math`'s 14 functions that IEEE 754 does not
# require correctly rounded (sin cos tan asin acos atan exp log log2 log10 cbrt pow atan2 hypot, both widths) were each
# one call into the platform's libm, which rounds them its own way in the last bit: over 5.8 million inputs, Windows'
# UCRT and emscripten's musl disagree on 343,547; UCRT disagrees with itself between a CPU with FMA3 and one without;
# and a release wasm build (`-Oz`, which links emscripten's table-free pow, log and log2) disagrees with a debug one.
# So under the key those functions come from this one copy, compiled into the program on every target, wasm included.
# tests/repro_math.d holds the bits on every leg.
#
# The inputs are checked against the sha256 recorded below, so the code can only change on purpose: to move to a new
# musl, change MUSL_VERSION and the hashes together, run this, and commit the header it writes.
#
# What this does to musl's code is mechanical; the arithmetic is untouched:
#   - every `#include` is dropped, and the helpers these files take from musl's internal libm.h are written once
#     (PRELUDE below, the same definitions renamed);
#   - every name the header defines gets kama's prefix (SPEC § C names). A name the files share becomes
#     `kama__musl_<name>`; a file's own static or macro becomes `kama__musl_<file>__<name>`, because 55 files that
#     were 55 translation units each have their own `N`, `T`, `top12` and `pio2_hi`, and here they share one;
#   - every definition is made `static`, so no symbol leaves the translation unit that includes the header;
#   - `__FP_FAST_FMA` reads as 0. clang predefines it on aarch64 whatever `-ffp-contract` says, and musl's log, log2
#     and pow then take a branch built on `__builtin_fma` whose last bit differs from the portable branch's. The
#     portable branch is what x86-64 and wasm run, so it is what every target runs. `TOINT_INTRINSICS` reads as 0
#     for the same reason (musl's default; an arch header may set it);
#   - `FORCE_EVAL` is empty, as emscripten's copy has it: it only raises a floating-point exception, which kama
#     does not expose, and never changes a result.
# A data header's `extern hidden const struct X {…} NAME;` becomes `struct X {…};`, and the .c that defines NAME
# defines it `static`. The files are written in an order that defines everything before its first use.
